#### Rotational background

For \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\), the combinatorial background is constructed using rotational background method. In the rotational background method, one of the daughter track is rotated by \(180^{\circ}\) in the transverse plane, and the resulting tracks are used to reconstruct the background. This rotation breaks the correlation between the two decay daughters and therefore the calculated invariant mass does not have signal but reproduces the shape of the combinatorial background. In this analysis, momentum vector of \(\Lambda\) is rotated by \(180^{\circ}\). Figure 3.9 shows the signal+background and combinatorial background using rotational method in the left panel and \(\Xi\)-signal after subtraction of background from signal+background in the right panel. Figure 3.10 left panel shows the signal+background and combinatorial background using rotational method and \(\Omega\)-signal after the subtraction of background from signal+background. The residual bump at lower invariant mass in Fig. 3.10 is due to the fake \(\Xi\) candidates being reconstructed because of fake \(\Lambda\) candidates. These fake \(\Lambda\) candidates are constructed due to the mis-identification of one of the decay daughters (\(\pi\),\(p\)). But this residual correlation does not affect the signal peak.

