then Eq. 3.11 becomes,

\[\frac{dN}{d\phi}=\frac{x_{0}}{2\pi}(1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}v_{n}^{obs}\cos(n\left[ \phi-\psi_{n}\right])), \tag{3.14}\]

where \(v_{n}^{obs}\) is the magnitude of flow coefficients. \(\psi_{n}\) is the \(n^{th}\)-order event plane angle, which is an estimate of true reaction plane angle (\(\psi_{R}\)). It is reconstructed for each event using azimuthal angle of the produced particles itself. Determination of the event plane angle is described in the following sub-sections. The event plane angle in general not always coincides with the true reaction plane in the experiments (because of the finite multiplicity N). If the error in the measurement of reaction plane is denoted by \(\Delta\psi_{R}\), then \(\psi_{n}=\psi_{R}+\Delta\psi_{R}\). We can obtain the relation between the observed flow coefficients \(v_{n}^{obs}\) and true flow coefficients \(v_{n}\) by averaging over all events (\(\langle\rangle\)):

\[v_{n}^{obs} = \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{n}])\rangle \tag{3.15}\] \[= \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{n}+\psi_{R}-\psi_{R}])\rangle\] \[= \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{R}]-n[\psi_{n}-\psi_{R}])\rangle\] \[= \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{R}]-n[\Delta\psi_{R}])\rangle\] \[= \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{R}]).\cos(n\Delta\psi_{R})\rangle+ \langle\sin(n[\phi-\psi_{R}]).\sin(n[\Delta\psi_{R}])\rangle\] \[= \langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{R}]).\cos(n\Delta\psi_{R})\rangle\] \[= v_{n}\left\langle\cos(n\Delta\psi_{R})\right\rangle.\]

Here, to arrive at Eq. 3.15, we have assumed that the \(\phi-\psi_{R}\) and \(\Delta\psi_{R}\) are statistically independent and the average sine term vanishes because of their reflection symmetry. These assumption are valid for the system with large multiplicity.

#### Determination of event plane

Reaction plane is defined as the plane formed by the beam axis (z-axis) and the impact parameter vector (perpendicular distance between the center of colliding nuclei). The angle between x-axis and the reaction plane is called the reaction plane angle (\(\psi_{R}\)). Experimentally, the impact parameter cannot be measured directly. Therefore it is not possible to find reaction plane angle for a collision in an experiment. The estimated reaction plane is determined from the produced particles, known as event plane. The \(n^{th}\)-order event plane angle \(\psi_{n}\) is calculated using [8],

\[\psi_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{Q_{ny}}{Q_{nx}}\right) \tag{3.16}\]

\[Q_{n}\cos(n\psi_{n})\ =\ Q_{nx}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}\cos(n\phi_{i}). \tag{3.17}\]

\[Q_{n}\sin(n\psi_{n})\ =\ Q_{ny}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}\sin(n\phi_{i}). \tag{3.18}\]

Where \(Q_{n}\) are the \(n^{th}\)-order event flow vectors, \(w_{i}\) are the weight and N is the total number of particles in a event used for flow vector calculation. The imperfect azimuthal symmetry of the TPC (affected by imperfections such as sector boundaries, non-uniform efficiency, temporarily dead channels, etc.) makes the reconstructed event plane distribution non-uniform (not-flat), and could bias flow measurements if not corrected for this effect. To correct this non-uniformity, three methods are widely used, namely phi weighting, re-centering and shifting. Figure 3.11 shows raw event plane angle distribution for various order.

In order to minimize the contribution to the event plane determination from effects and phenomena which are not necessarily correlated with the event plane, called non-flow effects, for example jets, only particles with \(p_{T}\)\(<\) 2 GeV/\(c\) were used in the calculation. A list of track cuts for calculation of the event plane angle are shown in Table 3.10.

