#### 3.6.4 \(v_{n}\) extraction methods

##### 3.6.4.1 \(\phi-\psi_{n}\) binning method

The \(\phi-\psi_{n}\) binning method is used for the extraction of flow coefficients \(v_{n}\) of identified particles. In this method raw yield (number of particles) of the chosen particle is measured in the bins of angle (\(\phi-\psi_{n}\)), where \(\phi\) is the azimuthal angle of the particle in the lab-frame and \(\psi_{n}\) is the \(n^{th}\)-order event plane angle. The measured distribution \(dN/d(\phi-\psi_{n})\) is fitted with the functional form of Fourier function to extract \(v_{n}\).

For particles like \(\phi\), \(K^{0}_{\rm s}\), \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\), where the signal consists of a mass peak sitting above a large combinatorial background as discussed in section 3.5, it is therefore necessary to remove the background from the signal to extract of raw yield of the particle. Raw yields extraction is done by two methods, function fit (for resonance like \(\phi\)-meson) and bin-counting (for weak decay particles \(K^{0}_{\rm s}\), \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\)).

An illustration of the raw-yield extraction using function fit for \(\phi\)-meson is shown in the Fig. 3.15. The \(\phi\)-meson signal after subtraction of mixed event background is fitted with a Breit-Wigner plus a \(2^{nd}\) order polynomial function for residual background shown by the red line. The invariant mass range chosen for fitting is \(0.995<m_{inv}<1.075\) (GeV/\(c^{2}\)). The \(\phi\)-meson yield is calculated by integrating the Breit-Wigner function only. This procedure is repeated for various \(p_{T}\) ranges and five \(\phi-\psi_{n}\) bins extend over \([0,\pi/n]\) in each \(p_{T}\) bin.

Raw-yields for weak-decay particles, \(K^{0}_{\rm s}\), \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\) are extracted using bin-counting. In bin-counting method, histogram bin content are added in a defined invariant mass range. An invariant mass range of \(\pm\) 20 MeV is taken for \(K^{0}_{\rm s}\) and \(\Lambda(\bar{\Lambda})\) around their mass values. For \(\Xi(\bar{\Xi})\) and \(\Omega(\bar{\Omega})\) bin counting is done in the invariant mass range of \(\pm\)10 MeV around their mass values from PDG. The same procedure is followed for all \(p_{T}\) bins and each \(\phi-\psi_{n}\) bin within a \(p_{T}\) bin for all centrality classesin U+U collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=193\) GeV. Figure 3.16 shows the raw-yield extraction using bin-counting method.

