#### Energy dependence of \(\phi\)-meson \(v_{2}(p_{T})\)

\(\phi\)-meson (\(s\bar{s}\)) has much smaller hadronic interaction cross section compared to other hadrons [34]. This would result in a smaller \(v_{2}(p_{T})\), if hadronic interactions dominate in the evolution of the medium. However, if the interactions are partonic dominated, then \(\phi\)-meson \(v_{2}\) will reflect the partonic collectivity. The measurements of \(\phi\)-meson \(v_{2}(p_{T})\) at different beam energies can be used to understand the effect of partonic and hadronic interactions of the medium.

Figure 4.13 shows the measurements of \(\phi\)-meson \(v_{2}\) as a function of \(p_{T}\) at mid-rapidity (\(|y|<1.0\)) in minimum bias Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=7.7\) - 39 GeV. The results for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=14.5\) GeV are new, while the results from other beam energies are from the Ref. [9]. The values of \(v_{2}\) at highest measured \(p_{T}\) bin for Au+Au collisions at 7.7 and 11.5 GeV are close to zero, which shows the dominance of hadronic interactions. Finite \(\phi\)-meson \(v_{2}\) at beam energies \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\geq 14.5\) GeV indicates the formation of partonic phase of the medium in heavy-ion collisions.

